<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" ?>
<conversations>
  <!-- No Load attribute, deliberately. ConversationLoader merges into an existing blueprint only
       when Load == 0, which is what an absent attribute means - vanilla's own Conversations.xml
       declares BaseConversation three separate times and relies on exactly that. "Replace" would
       discard every choice the base carries, the water ritual and [begin trade] included. -->
  <conversation ID="BaseConversation">
    <!-- A <choice> whose parent is <conversation> gets Distribute="Start" assigned automatically
         (ConversationXMLBlueprint, on reading the node), which is how it reaches the start node of
         every conversation in the game. The same mechanism carries vanilla's water ritual at
         Ordinal 980 and [begin trade] at 990.

         Target="TellName" is vanilla's own node, declared in BaseConversation and inherited
         everywhere, so the reply is Freehold's text rather than mine.

         The question pool is one <text> with ~ separators: IConversationElement.Prepare calls
         GetRandomSubstring('~'), so one is drawn per asking. The first six are vanilla's, kept
         verbatim; the rest are this fork's. -->
    <choice ID="Vixy_AskName" Target="TellName" Ordinal="10000">
      <text>
        What is your name?~
        What is your name, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        What may I call you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        What are you called, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        I am =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=. What is your name?~
        I am =name=, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=. What may I call you?~
        How may I address you, =pronouns.formalAddressTerm=?~
        By what name are you known?~
        What name do you carry?~
        What do your people call you?~
        I am =name=. What are you called?
      </text>
      <part Name="Vixy_AskName" />
    </choice>
  </conversation>

  <!-- Argyve's knickknack quest, both stages. Vanilla's <part Name="GiveArtifact" /> lists every
       artifact I am carrying with no importance check of any kind, so this swaps it for one that
       leaves out the ones I marked. See #570.

       The choice IDs are derived rather than written: a <choice> with no ID takes its Target plus
       the element name, so Target="GiveKnickknack" is addressable as GiveKnickknackChoice and
       Target="Impressive" as ImpressiveChoice. The part node's ID is its Name.

       Remove-then-add rather than Load="Replace": Replace matches on the ID, and a replacement
       node's ID depends on whether the reader saw ID or Name first, which is attribute order and
       not something to depend on. Two unambiguous operations instead of one subtle one. -->
  <conversation ID="Argyve">
    <start ID="StartHasFetch1">
      <choice ID="GiveKnickknackChoice">
        <part ID="GiveArtifact" Load="Remove" />
        <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_GiveArtifact" />
      </choice>
    </start>
    <start ID="StartHasFetch2">
      <choice ID="ImpressiveChoice">
        <part ID="GiveArtifact" Load="Remove" />
        <part Load="Add" Name="Vixy_GiveArtifact" />
      </choice>
    </start>
  </conversation>
</conversations>
